    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d290">

                <pb facs="0291" n="279" sameAs="frus:frus1941v01/pg279"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">874.00/628: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Bulgaria</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Earle</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sofia</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-01-14T16:00:00+02:00">January 14, 1941—4
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 8:50 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>14. The German-controlled newspapers here four times in the last week were about
                    to publish vicious articles against President Roosevelt. The Foreign
                        Minister<anchor n="21" xml:id="d290-f1"/> personally had every word of them
                    barred from publication. Only one anti-American article in a small weekly
                    escaped him.</p>
                <p>The censorship is under the Minister of the Interior, Gabrovsky, who is
                    pro-German. There is a struggle going on between him and the Foreign Minister
                    with the outcome uncertain.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Earle</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d290-f1" n="21" xml:id="d290-f1-content">
                <p>Ivan Popov.</p>
            </note>
